\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {16}{\ignorespaces Geometry of the generated clips against their ground truth (epoch 100, the 3{,}899 validation clips and the same frozen samples as Table~\ref {tab:repcontent}; official codec; bl = the rig's mean rest bone length). Last column: share of clips on which the \textsc  {TopX}{}-17{} sample is closer to the ground truth than the 13-channel sample (ties count for neither). Every row uses all 3{,}899 clips except foot slide, which each stream computes under its own contact flags and which is therefore compared only on the 3610 clips where all three are defined; over all 3{,}899 clips the streams flag contact on a similar share of frames --- 0.58 for \textsc  {TopX}{}-17{}, 0.56 for the 13-channel layout, 0.57 for the 303M model and 0.59 in the real clips --- so the lower sliding is not a matter of flagging fewer frames. Epochs 50 and 75 give the same picture. The 303M column is the main model's own frozen samples (the checkpoint of Table~\ref {tab:main}), decoded through the same stream and scored on the same 3{,}899 clips; it is read against the ground truth clip for clip exactly as the 36M column is, and it is closer to the ground truth than the 36M pilot on the FK--pose gap (95.4\% of clips), bone-length error (96.3\%), foot slide (65.8\%) and internal articulation (72.9\%), and farther from it on root displacement, root path length and the two jitter rows. The \textsc  {TopX}{}-17{} closer column compares the two 36M layouts, which is what that comparison is about. Foot slide is the one reference that depends on which pair of arms supplies the contact frames: 2.02 against the 13-channel arm, 2.03 against the 303M model.}}{23}{table.16}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:physical}{{16}{23}{Geometry of the generated clips against their ground truth (epoch 100, the \num {3{,}899} validation clips and the same frozen samples as Table~\ref {tab:repcontent}; official codec; bl = the rig's mean rest bone length). Last column: share of clips on which the \rep {} sample is closer to the ground truth than the 13-channel sample (ties count for neither). Every row uses all \num {3{,}899} clips except foot slide, which each stream computes under its own contact flags and which is therefore compared only on the \num {3610} clips where all three are defined; over all \num {3{,}899} clips the streams flag contact on a similar share of frames --- \num {0.58} for \rep {}, \num {0.56} for the 13-channel layout, \num {0.57} for the \num {303}M model and \num {0.59} in the real clips --- so the lower sliding is not a matter of flagging fewer frames. Epochs 50 and 75 give the same picture. The \num {303}M column is the main model's own frozen samples (the checkpoint of Table~\ref {tab:main}), decoded through the same stream and scored on the same \num {3{,}899} clips; it is read against the ground truth clip for clip exactly as the \num {36}M column is, and it is closer to the ground truth than the \num {36}M pilot on the FK--pose gap (\num {95.4}\% of clips), bone-length error (\num {96.3}\%), foot slide (\num {65.8}\%) and internal articulation (\num {72.9}\%), and farther from it on root displacement, root path length and the two jitter rows. The \rep {} closer column compares the two \num {36}M layouts, which is what that comparison is about. Foot slide is the one reference that depends on which pair of arms supplies the contact frames: \num {2.02} against the 13-channel arm, \num {2.03} against the \num {303}M model}{table.16}{}}
